then:
State only shows what Terraform thinks exists, so confirm in AWS that nothing is left billing you:

bash
aws eks list-clusters --region ap-south-1
aws rds describe-db-instances --region ap-south-1 --query "DBInstances[].DBInstanceIdentifier"
aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways --region ap-south-1 --filter Name=state,Values=available --query "NatGateways[].NatGatewayId"
aws ec2 describe-addresses --region ap-south-1 --query "Addresses[].PublicIp"
aws elb describe-load-balancers --region ap-south-1 --query "LoadBalancerDescriptions[].LoadBalancerName"

All five should return empty. An Elastic IP that isn’t attached to anything still costs money, which is why the fourth command is worth checking.

Two things are left, and neither is one of the three load balancers from your earlier destroy, so check what they are before you delete anything.

1. The load balancer aa85c5d9d388a4125a05f516dbd84abf

Its name is a Kubernetes-style hex string, so it was probably created by a LoadBalancer service at some point, maybe from an earlier attempt. Inspect it:

bash
aws elb describe-load-balancers --region ap-south-1 \
  --load-balancer-names aa85c5d9d388a4125a05f516dbd84abf \
  --query "LoadBalancerDescriptions[].[VPCId,DNSName,CreatedTime]" --output table
aws elb describe-tags --region ap-south-1 --load-balancer-names aa85c5d9d388a4125a05f516dbd84abf
If the VPC is not vpc-0cb1bd16ec43ab5e8 (your deleted VPC) and the tags don’t match anything you know, it’s another project’s. Leave it alone unless you’re sure it’s yours.
If you recognise it as a leftover from your practice and don’t need it, delete it:
bash
  aws elb delete-load-balancer --region ap-south-1 --load-balancer-name aa85c5d9d388a4125a05f516dbd84abf

2. The Elastic IP 35.154.114.137

Your NAT gateway’s IP was released by Terraform, so this is probably attached to something else. The most likely owner is the EC2 instance you’re working on (ip-172-31-4-128). Check:

bash
aws ec2 describe-addresses --region ap-south-1 --public-ips 35.154.114.137 \
  --query "Addresses[].[AllocationId,AssociationId,InstanceId,NetworkInterfaceId]" --output table
curl -s http://checkip.amazonaws.com
If the IP shown by checkip is 35.154.114.137, it’s your EC2 instance’s own address. Keep it, or you’ll lose your connection.
If InstanceId and AssociationId are empty (None), nothing is using it and it’s costing you money. Release it:
bash
  aws ec2 release-address --region ap-south-1 --allocation-id <AllocationId>

Everything from this Terraform project (EKS, RDS, NAT, VPC) is gone. These two leftovers weren’t created by Terraform, so I can’t tell you for certain whether they’re yours. If you paste the output of the two inspection commands, I’ll tell you which ones are safe to delete.
